ML_PAGE("l1-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 1 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Lecture 1 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l1\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"l1n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">39 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Chapter 1 - Fundamentals of Machine Learning, taught intuitively\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s0\" title=\"Chapter 1 - Fundamentals of Machine Learning, taught intuitively\">Chapter overview</button></li><li data-full=\"1. What is Machine Learning, really?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s1\" title=\"1. What is Machine Learning, really?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Rules vs learning from examples</button></li><li data-full=\"2. The most important definition: T-E-P\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s2\" title=\"2. The most important definition: T-E-P\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>T-E-P definition</button></li><li data-full=\"3. What exactly is the computer learning from?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s3\" title=\"3. What exactly is the computer learning from?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Features (X) and target (y)</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Don&#x27;t confuse the **task** with the **learning method**\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s4\" title=\"4. Don&#x27;t confuse the task with the learning method\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Task vs learning method</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Supervised Learning - &quot;Here are examples AND answers&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s5\" title=\"5. Supervised Learning - &quot;Here are examples AND answers&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Supervised learning</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Tiny supervised-learning code example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s6\" title=\"6. Tiny supervised-learning code example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Code: fit and predict</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Unsupervised Learning - &quot;Here are examples. You figure out the structure.&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s7\" title=\"7. Unsupervised Learning - &quot;Here are examples. You figure out the structure.&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Unsupervised learning</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Classification vs clustering - very important distinction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s8\" title=\"8. Classification vs clustering - very important distinction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Classification vs clustering</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Reinforcement Learning - &quot;Learn through consequences&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s9\" title=\"9. Reinforcement Learning - &quot;Learn through consequences&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Reinforcement learning</button></li><li data-full=\"10. One problem can contain several ML methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s10\" title=\"10. One problem can contain several ML methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Several methods in one project</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Second dimension: Batch Learning vs Online Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s11\" title=\"11. Second dimension: Batch Learning vs Online Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Batch learning</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Online Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s12\" title=\"12. Online Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Online learning</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Tiny online-learning example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s13\" title=\"13. Tiny online-learning example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Code: fit vs partial_fit</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Out-of-core learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s14\" title=\"14. Out-of-core learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Out-of-core learning</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Third dimension: Instance-based vs Model-based\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s15\" title=\"15. Third dimension: Instance-based vs Model-based\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Instance vs model-based</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Instance-based learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s16\" title=\"16. Instance-based learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Instance-based (KNN)</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Model-based learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s17\" title=\"17. Model-based learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Model-based (learned equation)</button></li><li data-full=\"18. A surprisingly important point: ML is mostly about generalization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s18\" title=\"18. A surprisingly important point: ML is mostly about generalization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Generalization</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Overfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s19\" title=\"19. Overfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Overfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Why overfitting happens\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s20\" title=\"20. Why overfitting happens\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Causes of overfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Fixing overfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s21\" title=\"21. Fixing overfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Fixing overfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Underfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s22\" title=\"22. Underfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Underfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"23. The most important graph in the chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s23\" title=\"23. The most important graph in the chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Complexity vs error curve</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Bad ML isn&#x27;t always caused by bad algorithms\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s24\" title=\"24. Bad ML isn&#x27;t always caused by bad algorithms\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Bad data: too little</button></li><li data-full=\"25. Nonrepresentative training data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s25\" title=\"25. Nonrepresentative training data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Nonrepresentative data</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Poor-quality data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s26\" title=\"26. Poor-quality data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Poor-quality data</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Feature engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s27\" title=\"27. Feature engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Feature engineering</button></li><li data-full=\"28. What machine learning is actually good at\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s28\" title=\"28. What machine learning is actually good at\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>When to use ML</button></li><li data-full=\"29. What an actual ML project looks like\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s29\" title=\"29. What an actual ML project looks like\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>The ML project loop</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Now the frameworks: KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s30\" title=\"30. Now the frameworks: KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Frameworks overview</button></li><li data-full=\"31. KDD - &quot;Turn raw data into knowledge&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s31\" title=\"31. KDD - &quot;Turn raw data into knowledge&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>KDD</button></li><li data-full=\"32. CRISP-DM - &quot;Solve the business problem, not merely the ML problem&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s32\" title=\"32. CRISP-DM - &quot;Solve the business problem, not merely the ML problem&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>CRISP-DM</button></li><li data-full=\"33. CRISP-DM intuitively using a bank example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s33\" title=\"33. CRISP-DM intuitively using a bank example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>CRISP-DM bank fraud example</button></li><li data-full=\"34. SEMMA - think &quot;analytics pipeline&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s34\" title=\"34. SEMMA - think &quot;analytics pipeline&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>SEMMA</button></li><li data-full=\"35. KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA - understand, don&#x27;t memorize blindly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s35\" title=\"35. KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA - understand, don&#x27;t memorize blindly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Put the entire chapter into one mental model\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s36\" title=\"36. Put the entire chapter into one mental model\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Whole chapter: pass/fail example</button></li><li data-full=\"37. How I would answer scenario questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s37\" title=\"37. How I would answer scenario questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Scenario answer checklist</button></li><li data-full=\"The chapter in one sentence\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s38\" title=\"The chapter in one sentence\">Chapter summary</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 1 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with code, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>39 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Chapter 1 - Fundamentals of Machine Learning, taught intuitively</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter is really trying to answer four questions:</p>\n<p><strong>What does it mean for a machine to \"learn\"? What kinds of learning exist? Why do ML systems fail? And how do we structure an ML project from beginning to end?</strong></p>\n<p>That matches the chapter's stated outcomes: explain ML, identify what it can do and its learning types, and evaluate ML frameworks.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What is Machine Learning, really?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_tradml\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Same goal, different author of the rules: in ML the algorithm produces them from data and desired outcomes.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Forget algorithms for a moment.</p>\n<p>Imagine you're writing a program to decide whether someone is likely to buy a house.</p>\n<p>With <strong>traditional programming</strong>, you invent the rules:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">IF income &gt; 8000\nAND age &gt; 30\nAND savings &gt; 100000\nTHEN likely_to_buy = True\n</code></pre>\n<p>The problem is obvious: reality doesn't cooperate with neat little <code>IF</code> statements.</p>\n<p>Maybe somebody earns RM6,000 but inherited money. Maybe somebody earns RM20,000 but has huge debt. Eventually you end up with 15,000 rules and a programmer questioning their career choices.</p>\n<p>Machine learning changes the idea.</p>\n<p>Instead of:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Humans discover the rules → computer executes them</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>we do:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Give the computer examples → algorithm discovers useful rules/patterns</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture summarizes this contrast as:</p>\n<p><strong>Traditional programming</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Rules} + \\text{Data} \\rightarrow \\text{Output}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Machine learning</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data} + \\text{Desired Outcome} \\rightarrow \\text{Learned Model}$$</div>\n<p>So a useful mental definition is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Machine learning is learning a useful relationship from examples rather than explicitly programming every rule.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture gives both Arthur Samuel's classic definition and Tom Mitchell's more engineering-oriented definition.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. The most important definition: T-E-P</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Tom Mitchell's definition looks unnecessarily academic until you understand it:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Task}\\ (T) + \\text{Experience}\\ (E) + \\text{Performance}\\ (P)}$$</div>\n<p>A system learns if its <strong>performance P</strong> on some <strong>task T</strong> improves through <strong>experience E</strong>.</p>\n<p>Think of learning to play golf.</p>\n<p><strong>T = Task</strong></p>\n<p>Hit the ball toward the target.</p>\n<p><strong>E = Experience</strong></p>\n<p>Thousands of swings.</p>\n<p><strong>P = Performance</strong></p>\n<p>Distance from target, fairways hit, score, etc.</p>\n<p>If after 1,000 swings you're better than after your first 10 swings, you learned.</p>\n<p>ML is conceptually the same.</p>\n<p>Suppose we're predicting house prices:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$T = \\text{predict house price}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E = \\text{historical houses and their prices}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P = \\text{prediction error}$$</div>\n<p>Maybe our model originally predicts:</p>\n<p>Actual price = RM500,000<br />\nPrediction = RM650,000</p>\n<p>Error = RM150,000.</p>\n<p>After training:</p>\n<p>Actual = RM500,000<br />\nPrediction = RM510,000</p>\n<p>Error = RM10,000.</p>\n<p>Performance improved using experience.</p>\n<p>That is machine learning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. What exactly is the computer learning from?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture introduces another extremely important word:</p>\n<h4>Feature</h4>\n<p>A <strong>feature</strong> is simply some information describing an example.</p>\n<p>For a house:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">size = 2000 sq ft\nbedrooms = 4\nlocation = Ipoh\nage = 8 years\n</code></pre>\n<p>Those are features.</p>\n<p>Usually we represent them as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X = \\text{features}$$</div>\n<p>and the thing we want to predict as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$y = \\text{target}$$</div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X =\n[\\text{size, bedrooms, age}]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$y = \\text{house price}$$</div>\n<p>A single row might be:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2000, 4, 8  →  RM550,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>A dataset contains thousands of these examples.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes each data sample as containing multiple attributes/features and emphasizes that the task should first be formulated concretely.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Don't confuse the <strong>task</strong> with the <strong>learning method</strong></span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_types_tree\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Ask about the learning signal first, then about the shape of the target.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is one of the places students often muddle everything together.</p>\n<p>There are things ML can <strong>do</strong>, such as:</p>\n<p>classification, regression, clustering, anomaly detection and translation.</p>\n<p>But then there are different <strong>ways of learning</strong>.</p>\n<p>The chapter actually classifies ML along <strong>three separate dimensions</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Possible answers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Does the machine receive correct answers while learning?</td>\n<td>Supervised / Unsupervised / Reinforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How does new data arrive?</td>\n<td>Batch / Online</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How does it generalize?</td>\n<td>Instance-based / Model-based</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This classification appears explicitly in the lecture.</p>\n<p>This means these categories are <strong>not alternatives to one another</strong>.</p>\n<p>A system could simultaneously be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>supervised + batch + model-based</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example, a house-price regression model trained once every month.</p>\n<p>That is a very useful way to organize this whole chapter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Supervised Learning - \"Here are examples AND answers\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine I'm teaching you to identify fruit.</p>\n<p>I show you:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[apple picture] → Apple\n[orange picture] → Orange\n[apple picture] → Apple\n[orange picture] → Orange\n</code></pre>\n<p>You aren't merely seeing fruit.</p>\n<p>You're seeing:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Input} + \\text{Correct Answer}$$</div>\n<p>That is <strong>labelled data</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines supervised learning as learning from labelled data.</p>\n<p>Mathematically:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(X,y)$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X = \\text{inputs}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$y = \\text{known correct answer}$$</div>\n<p>The model tries to learn:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$f(X)\\approx y$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Classification</h4>\n<p>Classification predicts a <strong>category</strong>.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Email → Spam\nEmail → Not Spam\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Transaction → Fraud\nTransaction → Legitimate\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Image → Cat\nImage → Dog\n</code></pre>\n<p>The output is not primarily a measurement.</p>\n<p>It's a <strong>class</strong>.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$f(X) \\rightarrow \\text{category}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Regression</h4>\n<p>Regression predicts a <strong>numeric quantity</strong>.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">House information → RM650,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Weather information → 32.4°C\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Customer information → RM1,245 expected spending\n</code></pre>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$f(X)\\rightarrow \\text{number}$$</div>\n<p>So the easiest test is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Is my target a category or a quantity?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Category → classification.</p>\n<p>Quantity → regression.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Tiny supervised-learning code example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">X = [\n    [1000, 2],\n    [1200, 2],\n    [1800, 3],\n    [2500, 4]\n]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">feature 1 = house size\nfeature 2 = number of bedrooms\n</code></pre>\n<p>And:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">y = [250000, 300000, 450000, 650000]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then a basic regression model could be:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X, y)\n\nprediction = model.predict([[2000, 3]])\n\nprint(prediction)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The important part is not memorizing the code.</p>\n<p>Understand:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.fit(X, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Learn the relationship between X and y.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.predict(...)\n</code></pre>\n<p>means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Use the learned relationship on a new case.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's most supervised ML stripped of all the marketing jargon.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Unsupervised Learning - \"Here are examples. You figure out the structure.\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_clusters3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Unsupervised learning in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · Unlabelled customers in 3D (spend, visits, basket size). Run k-means and watch groups appear without any labels.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Now I remove the labels.</p>\n<p>I give the computer:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Customer A\nCustomer B\nCustomer C\nCustomer D\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>but I never tell it:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Customer A = Budget shopper\nCustomer B = Premium shopper\n</code></pre>\n<p>There is no known $y$.</p>\n<p>Only:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X$$</div>\n<p>The computer searches for structure inside $X$.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes unsupervised learning as finding similarities and patterns within an unlabeled dataset.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Clustering</h4>\n<p>Suppose customers naturally look like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Group 1:\nlow spending\nfrequent purchases\ndiscount heavy\n\nGroup 2:\nhigh spending\nfew purchases\nluxury products\n\nGroup 3:\nmedium spending\nonline only\n</code></pre>\n<p>Nobody gave the machine those groups.</p>\n<p>It discovered them.</p>\n<p>That's <strong>clustering</strong>.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Supervised learning asks:<br />\n<strong>\"What is the answer?\"</strong></p>\n<p>Unsupervised learning asks:<br />\n<strong>\"Is there some hidden structure here?\"</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Classification vs clustering - very important distinction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose Spotify wants to group listeners.</p>\n<h5>Classification</h5>\n<p>Spotify already knows:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Rock fan\nPop fan\nClassical fan\n</code></pre>\n<p>and trains a model to identify them.</p>\n<p>That's supervised.</p>\n<h5>Clustering</h5>\n<p>Spotify says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We don't actually know what useful customer groups exist. Find patterns.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Algorithm discovers:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Cluster A\nCluster B\nCluster C\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then humans examine the clusters afterward.</p>\n<p>That's unsupervised.</p>\n<p>Same data.</p>\n<p>Very different question.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Reinforcement Learning - \"Learn through consequences\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Reinforcement learning changes the setup completely.</p>\n<p>Instead of saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Here is the correct answer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>we say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Try something. I'll tell you whether that outcome was good.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture uses the example of teaching a dog to fetch: successful behaviour earns reward, so behaviour that produces reward becomes more likely.</p>\n<p>The basic loop is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"State → Action → Reward → New State\"><span>State</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Action</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Reward</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>New State</span></div>\n<p>Imagine a game.</p>\n<p>The AI sees:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">enemy ahead\nhealth = 25%\nammo = low\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's its <strong>state</strong>.</p>\n<p>It chooses:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">attack\nhide\nrun\nheal\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's an <strong>action</strong>.</p>\n<p>The environment then produces something like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">kill enemy → +10 reward\ndie → -100 reward\n</code></pre>\n<p>Over many attempts, the agent learns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Which action tends to maximize future reward?}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h5>Small terminology note</h5>\n<p>The slide describes reinforcement learning as \"also called semi-supervised.\"</p>\n<p>In standard modern ML terminology, <strong>reinforcement learning and semi-supervised learning are separate concepts</strong>. For this chapter, the important idea to retain is the lecture's actual RL mechanism:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>learning through experimentation, rewards and consequences.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. One problem can contain several ML methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine Netflix.</p>\n<p>It could use:</p>\n<p><strong>Supervised regression</strong></p>\n<p>Predict:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Expected rating}=4.6$$</div>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong></p>\n<p>Predict:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Will user watch movie? Yes/No}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Clustering</strong></p>\n<p>Discover:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">crime-thriller fans\nrom-com fans\nanime fans\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Reinforcement learning</strong></p>\n<p>Experiment with recommendations and learn from subsequent user behaviour.</p>\n<p>This is why the lecture explicitly notes that one project may employ more than one ML method.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Second dimension: Batch Learning vs Online Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_batch_online\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Batch learns in big retraining events; online learns a little from every mini-batch.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Now forget supervised/unsupervised.</p>\n<p>We're asking a different question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>When does the model learn?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h4>Batch Learning</h4>\n<p>Imagine preparing for an exam.</p>\n<p>You receive the whole textbook.</p>\n<p>You study everything.</p>\n<p>Then you sit the exam.</p>\n<p>You don't learn new chapters while sitting inside the exam hall.</p>\n<p>That's essentially batch learning.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes batch systems as being trained on the available dataset before deployment; afterward, the model applies what it learned without continuously updating itself.</p>\n<p>Flow:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Data → Training → Model → Deployment\"><span>Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Training</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deployment</span></div>\n<p>If the world changes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"New Data → Retrain → New Model\"><span>New Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Retrain</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>New Model</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<p>A bank retrains its credit-risk model every month.</p>\n<p>January data arrives.</p>\n<p>Model remains unchanged.</p>\n<p>End of January:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">old data + January data\n↓\nretrain\n↓\nnew model\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's batch learning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Online Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Online learning means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The model can update incrementally as new observations arrive.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture describes individual observations or small groups called <strong>mini-batches</strong> being fed sequentially to the model.</p>\n<p>Instead of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000000\\ \\text{examples}\n\\rightarrow \\text{Train}$$</div>\n<p>you might have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000\n\\rightarrow \\text{update}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000\n\\rightarrow \\text{update}$$</div>\n<p>then another:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000\n\\rightarrow \\text{update}$$</div>\n<p>and so on.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Why would online learning be useful?</h4>\n<p>Imagine predicting fraudulent transactions.</p>\n<p>Fraudsters don't politely keep using the same strategy forever.</p>\n<p>Yesterday:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">stolen cards\n</code></pre>\n<p>Today:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">fake merchant accounts\n</code></pre>\n<p>Tomorrow:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">AI-generated social engineering\n</code></pre>\n<p>A model that can update from recent information may adapt more quickly.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Tiny online-learning example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Some models support:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.partial_fit(...)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of repeatedly rebuilding everything.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.partial_fit(batch_1)\nmodel.partial_fit(batch_2)\nmodel.partial_fit(batch_3)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Compare that with batch learning:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.fit(all_data)\n</code></pre>\n<p>That's the core difference.</p>\n<p>Don't memorize libraries yet.</p>\n<p>Understand the philosophy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Batch} = \\text{learn in large retraining events}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Online} = \\text{learn incrementally}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Out-of-core learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture introduces another concept that sounds more complicated than it is.</p>\n<p>Suppose your dataset is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2\\ \\text{TB}$$</div>\n<p>but your computer RAM is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$16\\ \\text{GB}$$</div>\n<p>Obviously:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2 TB &gt; 16 GB\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can't load everything simultaneously.</p>\n<p>So instead:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Load 1 GB\n↓\nTrain\n↓\nDiscard\n↓\nLoad next 1 GB\n↓\nTrain\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>That is <strong>out-of-core learning</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture explains that online algorithms can therefore also train on datasets too large to fit in main memory.</p>\n<p>Important distinction:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Online learning doesn't necessarily mean \"internet.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It means <strong>incremental learning</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Third dimension: Instance-based vs Model-based</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we're asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How does the system make predictions about something it hasn't seen before?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture summarizes this beautifully:</p>\n<p><strong>Instance-based:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which previous examples look like this?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model-based:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What general relationship can I learn from the examples?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This distinction is extremely useful.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Instance-based learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_knn\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Instance-based versus model-based</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Instance-based prediction looks at the nearest stored examples; the model-based line predicts from a learned rule.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Imagine you're deciding whether a new student is likely to enjoy Machine Learning.</p>\n<p>Instead of deriving some grand theory of human psychology, you ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which previous students are most similar to this student?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>New student:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">likes programming\nlikes statistics\nlikes problem solving\n</code></pre>\n<p>Find several similar previous students.</p>\n<p>If most of them enjoyed ML:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">predict → Enjoy ML\n</code></pre>\n<p>The model relies heavily on stored examples.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes this as essentially learning examples \"by heart\" and generalizing using a similarity measure.</p>\n<p>A classic example is:</p>\n<p><strong>k-Nearest Neighbours (KNN)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Prediction} =\n\\text{answer of nearest/similar examples}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Model-based learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of remembering examples individually, we attempt to learn a general rule.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{HousePrice}\n=\na(\\text{Size})+b$$</div>\n<p>Training determines:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$a=250$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$b=50000$$</div>\n<p>So the learned model becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Price}=250(\\text{Size})+50000$$</div>\n<p>For a house of size $2000$:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Price}=250(2000)+50000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM550,000}$$</div>\n<p>We no longer ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which house looks most similar?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>We ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What does my learned model predict?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture defines model-based learning as building a model from examples and then using the model to make predictions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. A surprisingly important point: ML is mostly about generalization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine this dataset:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Student 1 → Pass\nStudent 2 → Fail\nStudent 3 → Pass\nStudent 4 → Pass\n</code></pre>\n<p>A terrible model could simply memorize:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">if student == 1:\n    return &quot;Pass&quot;\n\nif student == 2:\n    return &quot;Fail&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Training accuracy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100\\%$$</div>\n<p>Amazing?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Ask it about:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Student 5\n</code></pre>\n<p>It has no clue.</p>\n<p>The whole purpose of ML isn't:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>perform well on examples you already know.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>learn something that remains useful on new examples.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's called <strong>generalization</strong>.</p>\n<p>And this leads directly into the biggest ML problem in the chapter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Overfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_overfit\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Overfitting and underfitting</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Raise the model complexity and watch training error keep falling while test error rises again.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Imagine five data points:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">•\n   •\n      •\n         •\n            •\n</code></pre>\n<p>A reasonable model discovers the overall trend.</p>\n<p>An overfitted model tries desperately to go exactly through every point:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">~\\/~~/\\_/\\/\\/~\n</code></pre>\n<p>It memorizes tiny irregularities.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines overfitting as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the model performs well on training data but does not generalize well.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This distinction is fundamental.</p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Training</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Unseen/Test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Good model</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">92%</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">89%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overfit model</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>99.9%</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>67%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That 99.9% isn't impressive anymore.</p>\n<p>It's suspicious.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Why overfitting happens</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture says overfitting becomes likely when the model is too complex relative to the amount and noisiness of available training data.</p>\n<p>Imagine five dots.</p>\n<p>A simple curve:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">────────╮\n        ╰──────\n</code></pre>\n<p>can explain the general trend.</p>\n<p>But you give the model enough freedom to draw:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">_/\\/\\/\\/\\_/\\/\\_\n</code></pre>\n<p>It can memorize every tiny accident.</p>\n<p>This is why:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More complex} \\neq \\text{automatically better}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Fixing overfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Conceptually there are three major solutions in your lecture.</p>\n<p>Make the model simpler.</p>\n<p>Give it more data.</p>\n<p>Improve/remove noise in the data.</p>\n<p>Why does more data help?</p>\n<p>Because weird coincidences become harder to memorize.</p>\n<p>Imagine observing:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2 customers\n</code></pre>\n<p>Both people who bought coffee happened to wear glasses.</p>\n<p>Model might think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Glasses} \\rightarrow \\text{Coffee}$$</div>\n<p>Now collect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">100,000 customers\n</code></pre>\n<p>That nonsense relationship probably disappears.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Underfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Underfitting is the opposite problem.</p>\n<p>Imagine trying to explain everything using an absurdly simple rule.</p>\n<p>Real relationship:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">      •\n   •     •\n •         •\n</code></pre>\n<p>Model:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">────────────\n</code></pre>\n<p>It simply cannot represent the pattern.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines underfitting as the model being too simple to learn the underlying structure.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Underfitting} = \\text{model too simple}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Overfitting} = \\text{model too adapted to training data}$$</div>\n<p>And somewhere between them:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Good generalization}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. The most important graph in the chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides shows model complexity against error.</p>\n<p>As complexity increases:</p>\n<p><strong>Training error usually keeps decreasing.</strong></p>\n<p>That makes sense.</p>\n<p>A more flexible model can fit training observations more closely.</p>\n<p>But <strong>test error</strong> behaves differently:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">high\n ↓\n ↓\n minimum\n ↑\n ↑\n</code></pre>\n<p>So you essentially get:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Underfitting → Sweet Spot → Overfitting\"><span>Underfitting</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Sweet Spot</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Overfitting</span></div>\n<p>This explains something beginners often misunderstand:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The model with the lowest training error is <strong>not necessarily the best model</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The goal isn't:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\min \\text{TrainingError}$$</div>\n<p>The real goal is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\min \\text{Error on unseen data}$$</div>\n<p>That's the heart of model evaluation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Bad ML isn't always caused by bad algorithms</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A major message from this chapter is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Bad data can destroy a perfectly good algorithm.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture identifies several problems.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Too little data</h4>\n<p>Many ML methods need a substantial number of examples, especially for complicated tasks like image and speech recognition.</p>\n<p>The computer can't magically learn patterns that aren't represented.</p>\n<p>If I show you one cat:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[one cat photo]\n</code></pre>\n<p>and say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Learn exactly what every possible cat looks like.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good luck.</p>\n<p>Same problem for ML.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. Nonrepresentative training data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This problem is even more important than dataset size.</p>\n<p>Suppose you're building:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A model predicting Malaysian household electricity demand.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But your training data comes exclusively from:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">large houses in Kuala Lumpur\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then you deploy it to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">apartments in Penang\nrural houses in Kelantan\nterrace houses in Ipoh\n</code></pre>\n<p>Huge dataset?</p>\n<p>Maybe.</p>\n<p>Representative?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly says that training data must represent the new cases where we expect the model to generalize.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Large Data} \\neq \\text{Good Data}$$</div>\n<p>You need:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Relevant} + \\text{Representative} + \\text{Sufficient}$$</div>\n<p>data.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Poor-quality data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a dataset says:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 182\nIncome = -RM70,000\nHeight = 984 cm\n</code></pre>\n<p>Your algorithm won't stop and say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"That seems a little odd.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It'll happily learn from rubbish.</p>\n<p>Hence the classic:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Garbage In} \\rightarrow \\text{Garbage Out}}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture discusses noise, errors, outliers and missing values, including possible solutions such as correcting/removing outliers or imputing missing information.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Feature engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Here's a subtle idea.</p>\n<p>Suppose I want to predict whether somebody will buy an expensive car.</p>\n<p>I give the model:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">shoe size\nfavourite colour\nnumber of vowels in name\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then I complain:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Why is my AI terrible?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Because my features are terrible.</p>\n<p>The chapter emphasizes that good features are crucial and defines <strong>feature engineering</strong> as involving feature selection, feature extraction and creating new features.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Feature selection</h4>\n<p>Keep useful existing variables.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Income\nDebt\nAge\nRandom customer ID\n</code></pre>\n<p>Customer ID probably has little real predictive meaning.</p>\n<p>Remove it.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Feature extraction</h4>\n<p>Combine existing information into something more useful.</p>\n<p>Instead of:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Total Debt\nAnnual Income\n</code></pre>\n<p>you could create:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{DebtToIncome} =\n\\frac{\\text{Debt}}{\\text{Income}}$$</div>\n<p>Often this new feature tells a clearer story.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Creating new features</h4>\n<p>Maybe you simply don't have information needed to solve the problem.</p>\n<p>Then the solution isn't:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use a fancier neural network.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It may simply be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Collect better information.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>ML sometimes has remarkably boring solutions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. What machine learning is actually good at</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture highlights several situations where ML becomes useful: problems requiring huge sets of hand-written rules, difficult problems without good traditional solutions, changing environments, and discovering insights from large or complex datasets.</p>\n<p>Here's the intuition.</p>\n<p>Imagine spam detection.</p>\n<p>Traditional programming:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">IF contains &quot;FREE MONEY&quot;\n    spam\n\nIF contains &quot;WINNER&quot;\n    spam\n\nIF sender looks suspicious\n    spam\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>Spammers change tactics.</p>\n<p>You update rules.</p>\n<p>They change again.</p>\n<p>You update again.</p>\n<p>Eventually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Rule 38,729\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is exactly where ML becomes attractive.</p>\n<p>Give it huge amounts of:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">spam\nnot spam\nspam\nnot spam\n</code></pre>\n<p>and let it learn statistical patterns.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. What an actual ML project looks like</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides diagram is much more realistic than the glamorous version of ML.</p>\n<p>Real ML isn't:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">data\n↓\nAI\n↓\nprofit\n</code></pre>\n<p>It's closer to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Raw data\n   ↓\nPreprocessing\n   ↓\nStructured data\n   ↓\nLearning algorithm\n   ↓\nCandidate model\n   ↓\nEvaluate\n ↙       ↘\nbad       good\n ↓         ↓\nrepeat    deploy\n</code></pre>\n<p>Meaning:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Most ML work is iterative.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You rarely train once and announce victory.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Now the frameworks: KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter introduces three major data-mining/ML process frameworks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{KDD}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CRISP}-\\text{DM}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{SEMMA}$$</div>\n<p>Don't memorize them as three unrelated lists.</p>\n<p>Think about their <strong>philosophy</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. KDD - \"Turn raw data into knowledge\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>KDD stands for:</p>\n<p><strong>Knowledge Discovery in Databases</strong></p>\n<p>The flow shown on the slides is basically:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Raw Data\n   ↓\nSelection\n   ↓\nPreprocessing\n   ↓\nTransformation\n   ↓\nData Mining\n   ↓\nInterpretation/Evaluation\n   ↓\nKnowledge\n</code></pre>\n<p>The destination tells you everything:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data} \\rightarrow \\text{Knowledge}$$</div>\n<p>Selection decides which data matters.</p>\n<p>Preprocessing cleans it.</p>\n<p>Transformation makes it usable.</p>\n<p>Data mining discovers patterns.</p>\n<p>Interpretation determines whether those patterns mean anything.</p>\n<p>The detailed lecture descriptions follow exactly this progression.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. CRISP-DM - \"Solve the business problem, not merely the ML problem\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This one deserves more attention.</p>\n<p>CRISP-DM stands for:</p>\n<p><strong>Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining.</strong></p>\n<p>The core stages are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Business Understanding\n        ↓\nData Understanding\n        ↓\nData Preparation\n        ↓\nModeling\n        ↓\nEvaluation\n        ↓\nDeployment\n</code></pre>\n<p>But the slides deliberately shows arrows looping backwards.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because ML projects are iterative.</p>\n<p>You may reach modelling and discover:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Our data is terrible.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Back to data preparation.</p>\n<p>You may evaluate the model and discover:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"This doesn't actually solve the business problem.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Back to business understanding.</p>\n<p>That iterative nature is part of the point.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. CRISP-DM intuitively using a bank example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a bank says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Build AI.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is not a business objective.</p>\n<p>A proper <strong>Business Understanding</strong> question would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Reduce credit-card fraud losses while minimizing legitimate transactions incorrectly blocked.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now that's measurable.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes Business Understanding as defining business objectives, translating them into an ML/data-mining problem, and deciding data and evaluation requirements.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<h5>Data Understanding</h5>\n<p>What do we actually have?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">transaction amount\nmerchant\nlocation\ntime\ncustomer history\nfraud labels\n</code></pre>\n<p>Are labels accurate?</p>\n<p>Are there missing records?</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically includes data gaps, relevance, quality issues and initial insights here.</p>\n<hr />\n<h5>Data Preparation</h5>\n<p>Clean things.</p>\n<p>Handle missing values.</p>\n<p>Transform variables.</p>\n<p>Create useful features.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{distanceFromUsualLocation}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{spendingVsTypicalSpending}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture includes missing-value treatment, feature identification, transformations and creation of new features.</p>\n<hr />\n<h5>Modeling</h5>\n<p>Try appropriate algorithms.</p>\n<p>Maybe:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Logistic Regression\nRandom Forest\nGradient Boosting\n</code></pre>\n<p>Tune parameters.</p>\n<p>Measure performance.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes exactly this algorithm-selection and tuning process.</p>\n<hr />\n<h5>Evaluation</h5>\n<p>Don't merely ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Accuracy = 99%. Good?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">99.9% transactions legitimate\n0.1% fraud\n</code></pre>\n<p>A model predicting:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">EVERYTHING = legitimate\n</code></pre>\n<p>already gets:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$99.9\\%\\ \\text{accuracy}$$</div>\n<p>and detects exactly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0\\ \\text{fraud}$$</div>\n<p>Brilliant.</p>\n<p>This is why evaluation has to connect back to the business objective.</p>\n<hr />\n<h5>Deployment</h5>\n<p>Now make the thing actually usable.</p>\n<p>Perhaps:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">transaction\n↓\nmodel\n↓\nfraud score\n↓\nblock/review/allow\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes usability, implementation and ensuring consumers understand how to interpret model outputs.</p>\n<p>This is why CRISP-DM is broader than merely:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Train model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. SEMMA - think \"analytics pipeline\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>SEMMA stands for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Sample},\\ \\text{Explore},\\ \\text{Modify},\\ \\text{Model},\\ \\text{Assess}}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter presents:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Sample\n↓\nExplore\n↓\nModify\n↓\nModel\n↓\nAssess\n</code></pre>\n<p>Sample means selecting a suitable subset.</p>\n<p>Explore means understanding distributions and relationships.</p>\n<p>Modify means cleaning, transforming and creating variables.</p>\n<p>Model means applying algorithms.</p>\n<p>Assess means checking reliability/usefulness on data not used for training.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA - understand, don't memorize blindly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_frameworks\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Highlighted bands show the steps the three frameworks share; CRISP-DM alone wraps them in business understanding and deployment.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Here's the best mental picture:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Framework</th>\n<th>Main way to think about it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KDD</strong></td>\n<td>How do we turn raw data into useful knowledge?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CRISP-DM</strong></td>\n<td>How do we solve a real business problem from understanding through deployment?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SEMMA</strong></td>\n<td>How do we systematically prepare, explore and model data?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>There is substantial overlap.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">KDD Preprocessing\nCRISP-DM Data Preparation\nSEMMA Modify\n</code></pre>\n<p>all involve broadly similar work.</p>\n<p>Similarly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">KDD Data Mining\nCRISP-DM Modeling\nSEMMA Model\n</code></pre>\n<p>are closely related.</p>\n<p>The slides summarizes these side-by-side visually, which is useful precisely because the frameworks use different labels for many comparable activities.</p>\n<p>The biggest conceptual distinction is that <strong>CRISP-DM explicitly starts from business understanding and ends with deployment</strong>, whereas SEMMA is more centered on the analytics/model-building process.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Put the entire chapter into one mental model</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine you're building an ML system to predict whether a university student will pass a course.</p>\n<p>Start with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$T = \\text{predict pass/fail}$$</div>\n<p>Experience:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E = \\text{previous student records}$$</div>\n<p>Performance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P = \\text{prediction quality}$$</div>\n<p>Because the answer is known:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supervised}$$</div>\n<p>Because output is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Pass / Fail\n</code></pre>\n<p>it's:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Classification}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose you train once per semester:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Batch Learning}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose the algorithm learns general relationships instead of merely remembering nearby students:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Model}-\\text{Based}$$</div>\n<p>Now data problems appear:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">missing attendance\nwrong marks\nstudents from only one programme\nirrelevant ID numbers\n</code></pre>\n<p>You deal with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">data quality\nrepresentativeness\nfeature engineering\n</code></pre>\n<p>Train the model.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training accuracy = 99%\nTest accuracy = 65%\n</code></pre>\n<p>suspect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Overfitting}$$</div>\n<p>If:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training accuracy = 60%\nTest accuracy = 58%\n</code></pre>\n<p>suspect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Underfitting}$$</div>\n<p>Then organize the overall project using something like:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CRISP}-\\text{DM}$$</div>\n<p>That's essentially the entire chapter connected together.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. How I would answer scenario questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of memorizing definitions, use this reasoning sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>What is being predicted/discovered?</strong> That tells you the task.</li>\n<li><strong>Are correct answers/labels available?</strong> If yes, think supervised; if no, think unsupervised; if decisions generate rewards, think reinforcement.</li>\n<li><strong>If supervised, is the output categorical or numerical?</strong> Category → classification; number → regression.</li>\n<li><strong>Does the model learn all at once or incrementally?</strong> Batch vs online.</li>\n<li><strong>Does it compare against previous examples or learn a general relationship?</strong> Instance vs model-based.</li>\n<li><strong>Does it work on new data?</strong> Compare training and test performance for over/underfitting.</li>\n<li><strong>Could the problem actually be the data?</strong> Check quantity, quality, representativeness and features.</li>\n<li><strong>What is the full project lifecycle?</strong> KDD, CRISP-DM or SEMMA depending on the framing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That's much more reliable than memorizing 40 disconnected definitions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The chapter in one sentence</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The deepest lesson here isn't \"ML has supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning.\"</p>\n<p>It's:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Use experience from data to learn patterns that generalize to new situations.}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Everything else in Chapter 1 supports that idea:</p>\n<p><strong>T-E-P</strong> defines what learning means.</p>\n<p><strong>Supervised / unsupervised / reinforcement</strong> describes the learning signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Batch / online</strong> describes when learning occurs.</p>\n<p><strong>Instance / model-based</strong> describes how predictions generalize.</p>\n<p><strong>Feature engineering and data quality</strong> determine whether useful patterns can actually be learned.</p>\n<p><strong>Overfitting / underfitting</strong> tell you whether the learned relationship is useful.</p>\n<p><strong>KDD / CRISP-DM / SEMMA</strong> organize the whole process into a practical project.</p>\n<p>And that is almost exactly how the final slide summarizes the chapter: definitions of ML; supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning; batch, online, model and instance-based approaches; then KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA.</p></div></details></div></details>");
